The attached image (Figure 5) clearly illustrates that Nano-11 nanoparticles significantly enhance the uptake of adsorbed antigens, such as ovalbumin (OVA), by bone marrow-derived dendritic cells (BMDCs). Plot (A) shows flow cytometry dot plots where cells treated with Nano-11+OVA exhibit strong positive signals for both Nano-11 and OVA, indicating co-uptake. Plot (B) further quantifies this by demonstrating a substantial right-shift in the fluorescence histogram for OVA in the presence of Nano-11, signifying much higher antigen internalization compared to soluble OVA alone.

The image significantly contributes to vaccine development by demonstrating Nano-11's ability to act as an efficient antigen delivery vehicle to dendritic cells. Enhanced antigen uptake by these critical antigen-presenting cells is fundamental for initiating robust adaptive immune responses, allowing for the design of more potent subunit vaccines that may require lower antigen doses and elicit superior, longer-lasting immunity against various pathogens.